meta {
  name: Update Assignment Weights
  type: http
  seq: 11
}

put {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users/assignment-weights
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Cookie: auth_token={{authToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "weights": [
      { "user_id": "{{userId}}", "campaign_type_id": "{{campaignTypeId}}", "weight": 50 },
      { "user_id": "{{secondUserId}}", "campaign_type_id": "{{campaignTypeId}}", "weight": 50 }
    ]
  }
}

docs {
  Sets the % share of new leads each user should auto-receive within the
  caller's org, PER CAMPAIGN TYPE (schema 1.49.0 — a user's sales-pool
  weight and hiring-pool weight are independent). Requires org_admin
  rank or above.

  Rules enforced server-side:
  - Every user_id must already be an active, in-range-rank member of
    iam.user_org_mapping for this org (same eligibility resolveAutoAssignedUser
    uses) — otherwise the request is rejected with 400.
  - Every campaign_type_id must belong to the caller's own tenant —
    otherwise 400 (never trust a client-supplied campaign_type_id as
    tenant-scoped).
  - Within EACH campaign_type_id present in the payload, weights must sum
    to exactly 100, OR all be 0 (which disables auto-assignment for that
    pool — new leads of that type stay unassigned, same as today). A
    payload mixing two types is validated independently per type.
  - Changes are NOT retroactive — only future unassigned leads (Meta sync,
    manual lead creation without an explicit assigned_user_id) are affected.
  - A weight of 0 always upserts a real row here (this endpoint only ever
    sets what's in its payload; it never deletes a type from anyone's
    pool — that only happens via the edit-user reconcile path).

  Example: one user at 100 and another at 0 (same campaign_type_id) sends
  every new lead of that type to the first user. Two users at 50/50 split
  evenly; on an odd lead count the extra lead goes to whichever user
  currently has the larger workload deficit (random tiebreak if exactly
  equal).
}
